<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">1973 oil crisis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1973">1973</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as first oil shock</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1973 oil crisis began in October 1973, when the Organization of Arab Petroleum Exporting Countries, led by Faisal of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, imposed an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/us-oil-embargo-against-japan">oil embargo</a> on countries that had supported Israel in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yom-kippur-war">Yom Kippur War</a>, first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. With monthly production cuts, the price of oil rose by nearly 300%, from US$3 to nearly US$12 a barrel, before the embargo was lifted in March 1974. The shock set off a worldwide inflationary spiral and the steepest economic contraction since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>, ending the long post-war prosperity of the West. A second oil shock followed in 1979.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By 1969 American domestic oil production was peaking and could not keep pace with demand from vehicles, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> never bought much oil from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. The Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries had been founded at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> conference on 14 September 1960. Arab producers had tried to use oil as leverage in 1956 and 1967, but most scholars agree the 1967 embargo was ineffective. On 6 October 1973 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> attacked the Bar Lev Line in the Sinai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> launched an offensive in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan Heights</a>, and on 8 October Faisal of Saudi Arabia said he was willing to use the oil weapon if necessary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">OPEC</a> summit in Kuwait City on 16 October 1973 the price of oil was raised from $3.01 to $5.12 a barrel. Faisal had opposed a total embargo and agreed to a 5 per cent cut on 17 October only under pressure from other Arab states, but an American arms lift to Israel worth $2.2 billion, though Israel had asked for $850 million, enraged him. Every Arab state except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> joined the embargo, and by November exports from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> to the West were down by 60 to 70 per cent.</p><p>The embargo was not uniform. Of the nine members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> faced a complete embargo, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> received almost uninterrupted supplies for refusing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the use of their airfields. Henry Kissinger flew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> on 7 November 1973, and by 18 January 1974 he had negotiated an Israeli troop withdrawal from parts of the Sinai. On 18 March 1974 Faisal ended the embargo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Most scholars regard the embargo as a failure because the targeted countries did not change their policies; Roy Licklider concluded that lasting changes came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">OPEC</a> price rise, while Daniel Yergin said it remade the international economy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> imported 90 per cent of its oil from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and faced its most serious crisis since 1945. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> a national speed limit of 55 miles per hour was imposed in 1974 and the Strategic Petroleum Reserve was begun in 1975. Western central banks cut interest rates, and the resulting stagflation surprised economists. The crisis encouraged exploration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and created a rift within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1973 oil crisis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
